And then everyone on board was left for dead.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.280
So, running through the numbers real quickly, Flint's ship here is roughly the size of Queen Anne's Revenge.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:13.200
One of the most famous pirate ships in all of history. One of the ones that belong to Blackbeard

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.440
That ship was about 100 feet, or 30 meters long.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:23.480
Based on this wide shot of both ships, 
 we see the cruise ship is six times longer and 4 times taller than the pirate ship.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.860
Meaning that it's gonna be about 600 feet, or 180 meters long.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.360
Modern cruise ships of that size usually have between seven hundred and a thousand passengers,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.600
and an additional three hundred crew members on board.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.420
So let's snowball it and say that a thousand deaths total are in this scene.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:44.340
Suddenly jumping our movie with a total of seventeen, up to a thousand and seventeen.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.900
Like I said in the beginning, all it takes is one scene and the numbers fluctuate dramatically.

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:54.800
And with that, we have ourselves a new front-runner in the contest for Disney's deadliest movie.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.980
I don't know if fictional massive losses of life are something to celebrate, but uh, ok.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.000
Having established a new leader,  I need a bit of a break.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.040
There's only so much early 2000's era Disney that I can take in anyone's sitting,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:10.340
So lets wash it off us like taking a cold shower with some much beloved Disney classics.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:15.180
Classics that are so brutal, I literally cannot watch them because they are so sad.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:18.240
That's right, we're talking about Bambi.

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.820
The universe "Bambi" establishes implies that any forest creatures from deer to rabbit,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.380
can talk, think, and make character-developing decisions

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.780
meaning that any animal that dies during the course of this movie is gonna get added into our kill count.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:35.540
And aside from the infamous scene of Bambi's mother being shot, there's also a dramatic forest fire scene.

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.880
Remember, this is what passed as children's entertainment.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.420
Having just gone through a forest fire here in California, where the fire burned literally across the street from my house,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:49.760
I've gained a sudden appreciation for how devastating and scary these sorts of fires can be.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.980
I assumed, that in "Bambi", all of those woodland creatures would quickly become crispy fried.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.200
But surprisingly, that could be further from the truth.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:02.280
National Geographic published in 2014 piece titled, "What Do Wild Animals Do in a Wildfire?"

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.620
Where seasoned bush firefighter, Gabriel d'Eustachio, claimed that he didn't usually see animals trapped in the fires.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:12.800
The article goes on to point out that the loss of life for animals in these situations

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.920
is probably smaller than we think.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.980
Something that's supported, by yet another article, from the US Fish and Wildlife Services.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:24.040
Because animals have such better senses than we do, they're able to evacuate areas on fire

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.340
long before it becomes an issue.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.240
And most deaths only come from old or injured animals.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.120
And that's an important fact for our purposes, not just because we love animals, but because in "Bambi",

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:36.320
Bambi IS the injured animal running away from the fire.

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.640
He just came off from a brutal head-to-head battle against another deer.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.620
And yet, he still manages to escape.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.800
Along with the birds, squirrels, and rabbits, that we see making it away from the plains.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.560
So how many died?

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.740
Well, while there aren't any significant studies on the numbers of wildlife lost during forest fires,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:56.620
The Rim Fire, in Yosemite National Park, the 5th largest in California's history,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:08:02.120
only saw about three-hundred and forty-five elk die, out of an estimated fifty thousand.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:04.102
There are also thirty-six mule deer,

00:08:04.102 --> 00:08:04.829
twelve moose,

00:08:04.829 --> 00:08:05.741
six black bears,

00:08:05.741 --> 00:08:07.680
and nine bison lost to the flames.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.460
And that was a huge fire, whereas in "Bambi", we can actually see the outer limits of the flames.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:18.060
So, let's overestimate, and say that Bambi's fire, is about 1/5 of the size of The Rim Fire.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.680
So about eighty animal casualties all together.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:22.880
Oh yeah, and lets not forget about mom. Eighty-one.

